.TH FCP 1 "September 2026" "fcp 2.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fcp \- faster copy with progress, identical file detection, and parallelism
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fcp
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fISOURCE\fR \fIDESTINATION\fR
.br
.B fcp
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fISOURCE\fR... \fIDIRECTORY\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBfcp\fP is a Linux copy tool with familiar \fBcp\fP invocation forms and
a smooth terminal progress display, identical-file detection, parallel copy
support, and sparse-file acceleration.

It supports a focused set of common \fBcp\fP options; it is not a complete
GNU \fBcp\fP implementation. Unlike GNU \fBcp\fP, \fB-P\fP means progress.
Use \fB-d\fP to preserve symbolic links or \fB-L\fP to dereference them.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-archive\fR
Archive mode. Equivalent to \fB\-d \-r \-\-preserve=all\fR.
Preserves all file metadata (modes, ownership, timestamps, and extended attributes).

.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-preserve\fR[=\fIATTR_LIST\fR]
Preserve specified file attributes. \fIATTR_LIST\fR is a comma-separated
list of attributes to preserve: \fBmode\fR, \fBownership\fR, \fBtimestamps\fR,
\fBxattr\fR, or \fBall\fR. If specified without an argument, defaults to
\fBmode,ownership,timestamps\fR.

.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-recursive\fR
Copy directories recursively.

.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR
Prompt before overwrite. Uses one worker so each response applies to the
displayed destination.

.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-no\-clobber\fR
Do not overwrite an existing file.

.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-force\fR
Remove existing destination files before copying.

.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Display names of copied files.

.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-no\-dereference\fR
Preserve symbolic links instead of copying their targets. This is the default.

.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-dereference\fR
Copy the files that symbolic links refer to, not the links themselves.

.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-symbolic\fR
Create symbolic links instead of copying files.

.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-update\fR
Copy only when the source file is newer than the destination file.

.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fIDIRECTORY\fR, \fB\-\-target\-directory=\fIDIRECTORY\fR
Copy all sources into the specified DIRECTORY.

.TP
\fB\-T\fR, \fB\-\-no\-target\-directory\fR
Treat DESTINATION as a normal path even when it names a directory. Cannot be
combined with \fB-t\fR and accepts only one source.

.TP
\fB\-\-remove\-destination\fR
Alias for \fB--force\fR.

.TP
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-progress\fR
Show progress during copy operations. Progress is enabled automatically when
standard error is a terminal; \fB-P\fR also forces it when standard error is
redirected.

.TP
\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR
Disable progress display.

.TP
\fB\-\-parallel=\fI[N|auto]\fR
Number of parallel copy workers.
.RS 4
.PP
\fBauto\fR (default): Automatically determine the number of workers
based on CPU count, capped at 8.
.PP
\fB1\fR: Sequential copy (traditional cp behavior).
.PP
\fIN\fR: Use exactly N worker threads.
.RE

.TP
\fB\-\-verify\-hash\fR
Use SHA256 hash comparison for all same-size files during identical-file
detection. By default, fcp hashes only files whose size and modification
time match.

.TP
\fB\-\-reflink=\fI[auto|always|never]\fR
Use reflink (FICLONE) when the filesystem supports it for instant
zero\-copy.
.RS 4
.PP
\fBauto\fR (default): Use reflink when supported, fall back to normal copy.
.PP
\fBalways\fR: Always attempt reflink (will fail on unsupported filesystems).
.PP
\fBnever\fR: Never use reflink.
.RE

.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Preview what would be copied without actually copying anything.
Shows the files that would be processed.

.TP
\fB\-\-speed\-limit=\fISIZE\fR
Cap aggregate copy speed across all workers. SIZE can be a number with optional suffix:
.RS 4
.PP
\fBK\fR or \fBk\fR: Kilobytes (1024 bytes)
.PP
\fBM\fR or \fBm\fR: Megabytes (1024*1024 bytes)
.PP
\fBG\fR or \fBg\fR: Gigabytes (1024*1024*1024 bytes)
.PP
.PP
Example: \fB\-\-speed\-limit=50M\fR limits copy speed to 50 MB/s.
.RE

.TP
\fB\-\-sparse=\fI[auto|always|never]\fR
Control sparse file copying. When active, fcp detects filesystem holes using
the \fBSEEK_DATA\fR and \fBSEEK_HOLE\fR extent search, copying only allocated
data blocks and skipping holes without writing zeros to disk.
.RS 4
.PP
\fBauto\fR (default): Automatically detect sparse files if allocated blocks
are smaller than logical file size.
.PP
\fBalways\fR: Always treat source as sparse and seek data extents.
.PP
\fBnever\fR: Disable sparse copy optimization.
.RE

.TP
\fB\-\-atomic\fR
Atomically replace the destination file upon completion. The copy is written
to a hidden temporary file in the destination directory, flushed with \fBfdatasync()\fR,
swapped via \fBrename()\fR, and followed by a parent-directory sync.

.TP
\fB\-\-exclude=\fIPATTERN\fR
Exclude files matching the specified shell glob PATTERN. Can be given multiple times.

.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.

.TP
\fB\-\-config=\fIPATH\fR
Path to configuration file. Default: \fI~/.config/fcp/config\fR

.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help message and exit.

.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Show version information and exit.

.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.PP
\fBfcp\fR reads configuration from \fI~/.config/fcp/config\fR (or the
path specified with \fB\-\-config\fR). The file uses a simple
key=value format:

.PP
.nf
    # fcp configuration file
    parallel = auto
    progress = auto
    color = auto
    verify_hash = off
    verbose = auto
    reflink = auto
    speed_limit =
.fi

.PP
CLI options override config file settings. The config file
settings override built-in defaults.

.SH IDENTICAL FILE DETECTION
.PP
\fBfcp\fR skips copying files that are already identical to their
destination. The detection uses a fast decision tree:

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Same inode and device: skip immediately (hardlinked files)
.IP \(bu 2
Different sizes: definitely different, copy
.IP \(bu 2
Same size and modification time: compare SHA256 before skipping
.IP \(bu 2
Same size, different time, \fB\-\-verify\-hash\fR: SHA256 compare
.RE

.PP
This approach is similar to rsync's strategy: use the cheapest
check first, escalating only when necessary.

.SH SAFETY AND PROGRESS
.PP
During recursive discovery, fcp shows scan activity instead of an unreliable
percentage. During copying, the terminal display shows aggregate transferred
and total bytes, rate, ETA, and parallel activity; the completed state remains
visible before the summary.
.PP
fcp detects source changes during copying and reports failures to preserve
requested metadata or extended attributes. It refuses a direct symlink as the
destination and rejects recursive destinations inside the source and
dereferenced symbolic-link cycles.

.SH PERFORMANCE
.PP
\fBfcp\fR is optimized for speed through several mechanisms:

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCopy file range\fR: Uses the \fBcopy_file_range()\fR syscall for
in-kernel transfers when available (Linux 4.5+)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBParallel workers\fR: Multiple threads can copy different files
simultaneously
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReflinks\fR: On Btrfs/XFS, uses FICLONE for instant copies
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLarge buffers\fR: 1MB copy buffers reduce syscall overhead
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSequential I/O hint\fR: Advises the kernel for optimal read-ahead
.RE

.PP
These optimizations depend on the Linux kernel and filesystem. In automatic
mode, fcp falls back when an optimization is unavailable; \fB--reflink=always\fR
fails if reflinks are unsupported.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Copy a single file:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp source.txt destination.txt
.fi

.PP
Copy a directory recursively with progress:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp \-r photos/ ~/backup/photos/
.fi

.PP
Parallel copy with 4 workers:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp \-\-parallel=4 documents/ backup/documents/
.fi

.PP
Dry run to preview what would be copied:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp \-\-dry\-run \-r src/ dest/
.fi

.PP
Copy with speed limit:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp \-\-speed\-limit=100M large_file.iso ~/backup/
.fi

.PP
Copy multiple sources into a directory:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt destination_dir/
.fi

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
If set to any value, disables colored output.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
Successful completion.

.TP
\fB1\fR
An error occurred.

